{
  "lessonActivities": [
    {
      "id": "lesson-1",
      "title": "10.1 Gerund vs Infinitive: Core Concepts",
      "content": "<p>Gerunds (verb+ing) and infinitives (to+verb) are used in different syntactic positions and with different verbs. Some verbs only take gerund, some only infinitive, and some can take both with minor or major meaning changes.</p>",
      "examples": [
        "I enjoy reading books. (gerund)",
        "I want to read the book. (infinitive)"
      ],
      "difficulty": 1,
      "audio": "lesson_1_gerund_vs_infinitive.mp3",
      "functions": ["Identify gerund vs infinitive", "Learn basic rules"],
      "tips": "Gerund often follows preference/emotion verbs; infinitive often follows intention/desire verbs."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-2",
      "title": "10.1 Common Gerund Verbs",
      "content": "<p>Verbs that take gerund include enjoy, avoid, finish, suggest, keep, mind, stop, admit, deny, miss. These verbs describe activities or mental states where the verb is treated as a noun-like action.</p>",
      "examples": [
        "She avoids waiting in line.",
        "He suggested taking an earlier train."
      ],
      "difficulty": 2,
      "audio": "lesson_2_gerund_verbs.mp3",
      "functions": ["Practice gerund verbs", "Use gerunds in sentences"],
      "tips": "Many gerund verbs are linked to experiences, preferences, or disliked actions."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-3",
      "title": "10.1 Common Infinitive Verbs",
      "content": "<p>Verbs that take infinitive include want, hope, plan, decide, intend, need, promise, offer, refuse, learn. Often these verbs refer to goals, intentions, or scheduled actions.</p>",
      "examples": [
        "They plan to start lessons next month.",
        "I need to finish my homework."
      ],
      "difficulty": 2,
      "audio": "lesson_3_infinitive_verbs.mp3",
      "functions": ["Practice infinitive verbs", "Use infinitives in context"],
      "tips": "Infinitives frequently express future-focused actions or desires."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-4",
      "title": "10.1 Gerund vs Infinitive: Meaning Shift Verbs",
      "content": "<p>Some verbs take both forms with meaning difference: remember, forget, stop, try, begin, continue. This lesson explains how meaning changes with gerund vs infinitive.</p>",
      "examples": [
        "I remembered locking the door. (past memory)",
        "I remembered to lock the door. (future reminder)"
      ],
      "difficulty": 3,
      "audio": "lesson_4_meaning_shift.mp3",
      "functions": ["Understand nuanced meaning", "Choose correct form"],
      "tips": "When in doubt, check if the action is completed (gerund) or intended/scheduled (infinitive)."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-5",
      "title": "10.1 Gerunds and Infinitives in Real-Life English",
      "content": "<p>Explore advanced patterns: expressions + gerund (no use, no point in, can’t help, be good at), to-infinitive used for purpose (to improve, to learn), and mixed cases in formal writing.</p>",
      "examples": [
        "It’s no use arguing with him.",
        "She studied hard to pass the exam."
      ],
      "difficulty": 3,
      "audio": "lesson_5_real_life.mp3",
      "functions": ["Use gerund/infinitive naturally", "Apply in conversations"],
      "tips": "Use gerund after expressions and prepositions, infinitive for purposes and goals."
    }
  ],
